{0 Mini Linear}

Mini Linear is the reference app of the repository, in [linear/]. It is a
small issue tracker: a list of issues, a page for one issue, a dialog that
creates an issue, and live updates. The data is mock data on the server. The
app is the test of Silica as a whole, not a product.

This page shows how the app is made. The app changes often, so this page
quotes only the parts that seldom change. Read the files in [linear/] for the
rest.

{1 The folders}

- [linear/app/shared/]: the code of the app. Dune compiles it two times:
  [linear/app/native/] for the server and [linear/app/melange/] for the
  browser. See {!page-recipes.json} for this setup.
- [linear/server/]: the server. It answers the Remote calls, keeps the issues
  in memory, and publishes changes on a Live topic.
- [linear/client/]: the browser entry point, the Tailwind input and the
  bundle rules. See {!page-recipes.tailwind}.

{1 The model}

The model is plain OCaml types in [linear_model.ml]. [[@@deriving json]] makes
the JSON functions, and [Silica_jsonkit.codec] makes a {!Silica.Codec.t} from
them. An issue is a record:

From [linear/app/shared/linear_model.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
module Issue = struct
  type t = {
    number : int;
    title : string;
    description : string;
    status : Issue_status.t;
    priority : Priority.t;
    assignee : User.t option;
    labels : Label.t list;
    created_at : int;
    updated_at : int;
    version : int;
  }
  [@@deriving json]
]}

The same file declares the endpoints and the topic. The server and the browser
use the same values, so the types of a call agree on both sides:

From [linear/app/shared/linear_model.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
let create_endpoint =
  Silica.Remote.declare ~rejected:Silica.Codec.string ~path:"/api/issues/create"
    ~input:(Silica_jsonkit.codec ~to_json:Draft.to_json ~of_json:Draft.of_json)
    ~output:(Silica_jsonkit.codec ~to_json:Issue.to_json ~of_json:Issue.of_json)
]}

{1 The routes}

The router is a machine whose states are the routes (see {!page-router}). The
routes are a plain variant, and each {!Silica.Router.val-route} says how one
constructor reads and writes a URL. [Issues] carries a record: the flag
[create] (the query
[?new=true] opens the create dialog) and the [view] of the list (filters,
grouping and order, see {{!section-features}Filters, edits and commands}).
[Route.map_query] turns the query values into that record. [Issue] carries the
issue number. [Not_found] takes any other path.

{@ocaml file=../linear/app/shared/linear_routes.ml[
open Silica

type issues = { create : bool; view : Linear_view.t }

let list = { create = false; view = Linear_view.default }

type route =
  | Issues of issues
      [@route
        Route.(
          root
          +? map_query
               (fun (create, view) -> { create; view })
               (fun { create; view } -> (create, view))
               (bool_query "new" ~default:false &? Linear_view.query)
        )]
  | Issue of int [@route Route.(s "issue" / int)]
  | Not_found of string [@route Route.rest]
[@@deriving routes]

let preload = function
  | Issue number ->
      [
        Remote.request Linear_model.issues_endpoint ();
        Remote.request Linear_model.comments_endpoint number;
      ]
  | Issues _ | Not_found _ ->
      []

let routes =
  Router.routes
    ~not_found:(function Not_found _ -> true | Issues _ | Issue _ -> false)
    ~preload route_routes
]}

[linear_app.mlx] renders the page of the current route, and a header with
links. A link is made with [Router.link], so a press changes the route with no
page load. The server gives status 404 when the route is [Not_found], and also
when the detail finds no issue: the detail raises [Action.Set_status 404].

The header stays at the same place for every route, so its components
([linear_create.mlx], [linear_me.mlx]) keep their state when the page changes:
this is the layout. The header link to the list gets [~current], so it has
[aria-current="page"] on the list. [preload] gives the calls of the detail page,
so a pointer on an issue link (or focus on it) starts them, and a press shows
the detail with no loading state. The page headings have
[data-silica-route-focus]: after a navigation, keyboard focus moves there.

{1 Components that own machines}

Each part of the page is a component with its own machine. The page puts a
component into its view as a node. The machine of the component starts when the
node shows, and stops when the node goes away.

- [linear_list.mlx]: the list of issues. It loads the list with a Remote call,
  then follows the Live topic.
- [linear_detail.mlx]: one issue. It gets the issue number as props from the
  route. When the number changes, it loads again.
- [linear_create.mlx]: the button and the dialog that create an issue.
- [linear_me.mlx]: the name of the visitor. It makes one Remote call and then
  stays in a final state.
- [linear_comments.mlx], [linear_comment.mlx] and [linear_composer.mlx]: the
  comments of one issue, one comment, and the field that adds a comment.
- [linear_unsaved.mlx]: shown by the detail while a title or description draft
  differs from the saved text. It holds a {!Silica.Source.block}: a navigation
  becomes a question (Discard or Keep editing), and Discard resumes it.

A parent talks to a child with props: a value that the parent gives with the
node. The child machine gets them in its update and its view. A child talks to its parent with [output]: a function that turns the
events of the child into events of the parent. See {!Silica.Node.child}.

{1 Remote calls}

A state that needs data runs a call: [Remote.call endpoint input on_answer] in
the [during] list of the state. The call starts when the machine enters the
state. The answer comes back as an event. If the machine leaves the state first,
the answer is dropped. The list waits in [Loading] for the first answer, and
goes to [Ready] or to [Failed]. [Failed] has a retry button. The answer is [Ok], [Rejected] (an application
error of the handler, such as a failed validation) or [Failed] (no answer). See
{!page-data.remote}.

On the first page load, the server runs these calls before it sends the HTML.
It writes the answers into the page. The browser uses those answers, so the
first paint in the browser is the same as the HTML of the server, and no call
runs two times.

{1 The Live topic}

The topic [issues] holds the full list of issues under one key. In [Ready], the
list runs a subscription: [Topic.subscribe issues_topic]. Each time the server
publishes a new list, every subscriber gets it as an event. When the socket
goes down, the data stays and its {!Silica.Status.t} is stale. When the socket
comes back, the server sends the full state again.

Experimental: Live updates can change. The checker and the runtime do not
agree yet on the order of the first update of a subscription.

The server publishes the list after each change, for example after a create
call. So all open pages show the new issue, also the pages of other visitors.

{1 The create dialog}

The create component has its own machine with the states [Closed], [Editing],
[Submitting] and [Failed]. The dialog itself comes from [silica-ui]: the app
gives the view of its form to [Silica_ui.Dialog.component], which adds the
focus trap, the Escape key, the outside press and the ARIA attributes.

The route controls the dialog. [?new=true] gives the [open_] props
({{!page-glossary}open request}), and the dialog reports each open change to
its parent. In [Submitting] the machine runs the create call. On success the
dialog closes, and the new issue arrives on all pages through the Live topic.
On failure the machine goes to [Failed] and keeps the form, so the user can try
again.

{1:features Filters, edits and commands}

{2 Filters and display options in the URL}

The list shows the issues that match the filter. The filter has four fields:
status, assignee, priority and label. In one field, an issue matches when it
has one of the selected values. All fields must match. The display options
group the list by status, assignee or priority, or not at all, and order it by
priority, by the last update or by creation.

The filter and the display options are query values of the [Issues] route,
for example [/?status=todo.done&group=assignee]. A value that the app does not
know gives the default of that query key. The URL prints the values in one
fixed order, so one view has one URL. When the user changes the filter, the
list does not change its own data: it replaces the URL. The router gives the
new view to the same list component as props. The list keeps its own state,
such as the closed groups, also when the Live topic sends a new list.

{2 Inline edit with an optimistic value}

On the page of one issue, the user can edit the title and the description, and
pick the status, the priority, the assignee and the labels. A change is a
value of [Change.t]:

From [linear/app/shared/linear_model.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
  type t =
    | Title of string
    | Description of string
    | Status of Issue_status.t
    | Priority of Priority.t
    | Assignee of User.t option
    | Labels of Label.t list
]}

The page shows the change at once. It keeps the data in a value of
{!Silica.Optimistic.Make} (see {!page-optimistic}): the last confirmed issues
and the changes that wait for the server. Each change goes to the server in its
own Remote call. The page runs one call for each waiting change: the calls are
compared by key after each step, so a new change starts a call and the other
calls keep running. The change stops waiting when the call answers, or
when the Live topic first sends issues that already have it. When the call
fails, the change goes away, the old value comes back, and a toast tells the
user.

Each change carries the version of the issue that the user saw. The server
saves the change only when that version is the current one. Otherwise it
answers with the current issue. Then the page shows that issue. When the
change was a title or a description, the text field opens again with the text
of the user and a note with the new value, so the user can save again.

{2 Command menu and shortcuts}

Ctrl+K or Meta+K opens the command menu of [silica-ui]. It can create an
issue, go to the list, open an issue by its title or its [ENG-n] name, and set
the status, the priority or the assignee of the focused issue. On the list, the
focused issue is the row of the cursor. On the page of one issue, it is that
issue.

The other keys: C creates an issue. F opens the filter. J and K, or the arrow
keys, move the row cursor, and Enter opens the issue of that row. S, P, A and L
open the pickers of the status, the priority, the assignee and the labels.
Ctrl+Enter or Meta+Enter sends a form, and Escape closes or cancels. A key
that the user types in a text field is text, not a shortcut: a shortcut with
one letter does not work there.

{2 Comments}

Each issue has comments. The page loads them with a Remote call and then
follows a Live topic, [comments], with the issue number as the key. A user can
add a comment and edit an own comment. The server takes the author from the
session.

{1 Checks}

The tests in [test/linear_check/] run the checker on the machines of the app
(see {!page-checker}).
The checker gives each call its sample answers and errors, and each topic its
sample states. In each state that it can reach, it checks the built-in
properties and the properties of the app.
